\begin{abstract}
Parameterized quantum circuits (PQCs) have shown promising results on molecular electronic property prediction, but the source of their advantage over classical baselines remains unclear. We show that this advantage traces not to quantum entanglement but to a classical mechanism: single-qubit rotations natively compute truncated Fourier series, providing a compact periodic basis that monotonic activations cannot efficiently express at low parameter budgets. To exploit this insight, we introduce \textbf{PRISM}, an $\mathrm{E}(3)$-equivariant message-passing GNN that replaces standard activations with periodic sinusoidal (SIREN) internal activations. At identical parameter count ($2{,}527$), PRISM matches or exceeds a 4-qubit PQC across all QM9 electronic targets and outperforms PaiNN on four of six properties. The periodic advantage persists to the full QM9 dataset ($N = 110{,}000$) and \textit{widens} with increasing model capacity. Our results establish periodic internal activations as a hardware-free inductive bias for molecular geometric deep learning.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}


Parameterized quantum circuits (PQCs) are leading near-term candidates for practical quantum advantage in machine learning~\cite{Kandala:2017vok, Cerezo:2020mtn}. Molecular property prediction is a natural application domain: molecules live in 3D Euclidean space with continuous $\mathrm{E}(3)$ symmetries that PQCs can naturally respect, and the underlying physics is quantum mechanical~\cite{satorras2022enequivariantgraphneural, thomasTensorFieldNetworks2018}. Indeed, when integrated into equivariant graph neural networks, shallow PQCs consistently outperform parameter-matched classical baselines on electronic orbital targets such as HOMO, LUMO, and the HOMO-LUMO gap.

But \textit{why} do they work? The dominant assumption is that quantum entanglement or Hilbert-space expressivity provides the edge. We show this is wrong. Through systematic ablation across 20 random seeds, we demonstrate that multi-qubit entanglement contributes negligibly to PQC performance on molecular targets. The actual mechanism is entirely classical: single-qubit rotations $R_Y(x)$, $R_Z(x)$ natively compute truncated Fourier series~\cite{Schuld:2020enb, Perez-Salinas:2019pjx}, supplying a compact periodic basis that standard monotonic activations ($\tanh$, SiLU) cannot efficiently express at matching parameter budgets. This is a representational capacity gap, not a quantum one.

This diagnosis is constructive: if the PQC advantage stems from periodicity, we can replicate it classically by placing periodic activations \textit{inside} equivariant message-passing layers. While SIREN~\cite{sitzmann2020siren} introduced sinusoidal activations for implicit neural representations in vision, and existing molecular architectures use trigonometric functions for \textit{input} encodings (spherical harmonics in TFN~\cite{thomasTensorFieldNetworks2018}, NequIP~\cite{batznerE3equivariantGraphNeural2021}, MACE~\cite{batatiaMACEHigherOrder2022}), no prior work has placed periodic activations inside the \textit{hidden layers} of equivariant graph networks. We introduce \textbf{PRISM}, an $\mathrm{E}(3)$-equivariant message-passing GNN that replaces monotonic activations with $\sin(\omega_0 \mathbf{W} \mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b})$ in its edge-processing MLPs. At identical parameter count, PRISM matches or exceeds the PQC on all electronic targets, without quantum hardware.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We isolate the mechanism of PQC advantage on molecular electronic targets: it traces to single-qubit trigonometric expressivity, not multi-qubit entanglement (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}).
    \item We introduce \textbf{PRISM}, the first equivariant message-passing GNN with periodic sinusoidal \textit{internal} activations, which matches or exceeds the PQC at iso-parameter parity and outperforms PaiNN on four of six QM9 targets (Section~\ref{sec:periodic_egnn}).
    \item We show that the periodic advantage \textit{grows} with model capacity and persists from $N = 1{,}000$ to the full QM9 dataset, establishing it as a structural inductive bias rather than a low-data artifact (Section~\ref{sec:param_scaling}).
    \item We provide a rigorous statistical methodology (factorial ablation, Fourier spectral validation, and Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction across 48 hypotheses) applicable to future QML benchmarking (Sections~\ref{sec:init_ablation}--\ref{sec:spectral_comparison}).
\end{itemize}

