\subsection{Main Results: Dequantization via PRISM}
\label{sec:periodic_results}

The results in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} provide a direct test of the quantum inductive bias hypothesis:

\paragraph{The Apparent Quantum Advantage Over Standard Baselines.}
Against the standard Classical Twin ($\tanh$-MLP) and parameter-matched EGNN ($2{,}497$ parameters), the 4-qubit PQC in Q-PRISM shows lower test MAE on electronic properties: errors drop by $11.9\%$ on HOMO ($0.303$ vs $0.344$\,eV), $12.8\%$ on LUMO ($0.628$ vs $0.720$\,eV), $10.5\%$ on Gap ($0.721$ vs $0.806$\,eV), and $15.5\%$ on Dipole ($0.764$ vs $0.904$\,Debye).
While early empirical evaluations of variational circuits frequently attributed observed gains to quantum Hilbert-space expressivity, foundational Fourier analysis of data re-uploading circuits~\cite{Schuld:2020enb, Perez-Salinas:2019pjx} demonstrates that they primarily generate truncated trigonometric polynomials.

\paragraph{Dequantization via SIREN Periodic Activations.}
However, when the classical twin is equipped with sinusoidal periodic activations and SIREN initialization~\cite{sitzmann2020siren} (\textbf{PRISM}), the apparent quantum advantage vanishes.
At identical parameter count ($2{,}527$ p), PRISM strictly outperforms Q-PRISM across all electronic properties (Figure~\ref{fig:dequantization_bars}):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{HOMO:} PRISM achieves $0.269 \pm 0.015$\,eV vs.\ Q-PRISM's $0.303 \pm 0.014$\,eV ($\Delta = 0.034$\,eV, $q_{\mathrm{FDR}} = 0.0017$). While statistically significant, this delta falls within the $0.043$\,eV chemical accuracy threshold and should be interpreted cautiously.
    \item \textbf{LUMO:} PRISM achieves $0.479 \pm 0.015$\,eV vs.\ Q-PRISM's $0.628 \pm 0.031$\,eV ($\Delta = 0.149$\,eV, $3.5\times$ chemical accuracy, $q_{\mathrm{FDR}} = 0.0052$).
    \item \textbf{Gap:} PRISM achieves $0.557 \pm 0.020$\,eV vs.\ Q-PRISM's $0.721 \pm 0.055$\,eV ($\Delta = 0.163$\,eV, $3.8\times$ chemical accuracy, $q_{\mathrm{FDR}} = 0.0126$).
    \item \textbf{Dipole:} PRISM achieves $0.711 \pm 0.020$\,Debye vs.\ Q-PRISM's $0.764 \pm 0.032$\,Debye ($q_{\mathrm{FDR}} = 0.0075$).
\end{itemize}
The primary evidence for classical trigonometric equivalence comes from LUMO and Gap, where PRISM's advantage over the PQC exceeds chemical accuracy by $3.5$--$3.8\times$. On HOMO, the delta ($0.034$\,eV) is within the chemical accuracy margin---consistent with our treatment of the entanglement effect ($0.006$\,eV) in Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}.
PRISM also improves over PaiNN ($2{,}721$ parameters) on four of six QM9 targets: HOMO ($0.269$ vs $0.283$\,eV), Dipole ($0.711$ vs $0.819$\,Debye), Polarizability ($1.415$ vs $3.446$\,Bohr$^3$), and Internal Energy ($130.6$ vs $446.0$\,eV).
We emphasize that this comparison is at iso-parameter budgets ($\sim 2{,}500$ parameters), a regime far below PaiNN's design capacity ($100$k+ parameters). At its natural capacity, PaiNN is expected to outperform PRISM on targets where vector equivariant channels provide structural advantage (see Table~\ref{tab:param_scaling}). The iso-parameter comparison demonstrates PRISM's \textit{parameter efficiency}, not its absolute superiority over mature equivariant architectures.
On the internal energy target ($U_0$), however, PRISM ($130.6 \pm 29.3$\,eV) underperforms the Classical Twin ($101.1 \pm 16.7$\,eV, $p = 0.13$, not significant). This target-specificity is informative rather than problematic: the periodic activation advantage is concentrated on electronic orbital properties with angular dependence (HOMO, LUMO, Gap, Dipole), while extensive thermodynamic quantities ($U_0$) that depend on smooth, monotonic energy summation do not benefit from periodic bases. This selectivity confirms that PRISM's improvement is \textit{mechanistic}---arising from periodic angular expressivity aligned with orbital physics---rather than a generic capacity improvement that would uniformly benefit all targets.

\paragraph{Architectural Comparison with PaiNN.}
While PRISM outperforms PaiNN on 4 properties, PaiNN maintains an advantage on LUMO ($0.398$ vs $0.479$\,eV) and HOMO-LUMO Gap ($0.470$ vs $0.557$\,eV).
This difference stems directly from architectural inductive biases: PaiNN uses coupled scalar-vector message passing~\cite{schuttEquivariantMessagePassing2021}, maintaining and updating 3D directional vector channels ($\vec{v}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times 3}$) across successive layers.
In contrast, our edge convolutions (both the 4-qubit PQC and PRISM) project 3D directional geometry into scalar invariant distances ($d_{ij}$) and mean angles ($\bar{\theta}_{ij}$), passing strictly scalar messages before updating coordinates via gated scalar scaling.
Continuous vector message passing provides richer representation capacity for non-local spatial directional interference that governs unoccupied orbital shapes (LUMO), explaining PaiNN's strength on these targets.
Unoccupied LUMO wavefunctions are more diffuse than occupied HOMO orbitals, requiring fine sensitivity to 3D directional arrangements. PaiNN's equivariant vector channels capture these directional orientations directly. By comparison, scalar models such as PRISM must reconstruct directional variations from averaged pairwise angles, limiting their capacity on unoccupied orbital targets.
Nonetheless, PRISM achieves superior accuracy on occupied orbital energy (HOMO), electrostatic dipole, and polarizability within an identical $\sim 2{,}500$ parameter budget.
\paragraph{Why Random Fourier Features (RFF) Fail.}
In contrast to SIREN, the Random Fourier Feature twin ($2{,}515$ parameters) achieves inferior accuracy across all targets ($0.424$\,eV HOMO, $0.775$\,eV LUMO, $0.846$\,eV Gap, $3.903$\,Bohr$^3$ $\alpha$).
This gap reflects a key architectural difference: RFF projects inputs through a fixed Gaussian matrix $\mathbf{B}$~\cite{tancik2020fourier, landman2023approximating}.
In molecular systems, interatomic bond angles and orbital hybridization vary dynamically across different chemical elements and coordination geometries.
SIREN allows the frequency weights $\mathbf{W}_1$ to be learned end-to-end via backpropagation, dynamically tuning the periodic spectrum to the electronic structure of the molecule.

