\paragraph{Toward PRISM-PaiNN.}
\label{sec:prism_painn}
The complementary performance profiles of PRISM (superior on HOMO, Dipole, Polarizability) and PaiNN (superior on LUMO, Gap) suggest a natural architectural synthesis: replacing the SiLU activations in PaiNN's scalar interaction branch with SIREN periodic activations while preserving the vector equivariant update channels unchanged. Since the periodic activation acts only on the invariant scalar pathway, this requires no modification to PaiNN's equivariance guarantees. We discuss this hybrid architecture in detail in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} (Limitations and Future Work).


\subsection{Statistical Rigor: Multiple Comparisons and FDR}
\label{sec:fdr_analysis}

A critical methodological concern in benchmarking multi-target molecular datasets is the risk of false discoveries arising from uncorrected multiple hypothesis testing.
Testing 8 models across 6 physical targets generates $m = 8 \times 6 = 48$ pairwise statistical comparisons, where each model is compared against its designated classical baseline (Classical Twin $\tanh$-MLP) via paired $t$-tests across 5 random seeds. Specifically, the 48 hypotheses correspond to all pairwise model-vs-baseline comparisons for each of the 6 QM9 targets (HOMO, LUMO, Gap, Dipole, Polarizability, $U_0$), testing whether each model's MAE differs significantly from the Classical Twin.

To address this, we apply two standard multiplicity control frameworks:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Family-Wise Error Rate (FWER) Control via Bonferroni:}
    $p_{\mathrm{Bonf}} = \min(1.0, m \cdot p_{\mathrm{raw}})$, guaranteeing strict FWER $\le 0.05$.
    \item \textbf{False Discovery Rate (FDR) Control via Benjamini-Hochberg (BH):}
    $q_{\mathrm{FDR}} = \min_{k \ge i} \left(\frac{m}{k} p_{(k)}\right)$, controlling the expected proportion of false positives at level $\alpha = 0.05$.
\end{enumerate}

