\subsection{Mechanism of Quantum Advantage}
\label{sec:mechanism}

A natural objection to quantum advantage claims is that a classical MLP with $\tanh$ output activation produces identically bounded outputs $\in (-1, 1)$, potentially replicating any inductive bias from Pauli observables~\cite{bowles2024better}. To isolate the \textit{active mechanism} of quantum advantage, we conduct three complementary analyses.


\subsubsection{Four-Tier Iso-Architecture Entanglement Ablation}

Following the benchmarking methodology of Bowles et al.~\cite{bowles2024better}, we construct four models at strict iso-parameter count ($2{,}527$ parameters) sharing identical GNN backbone, embedding, pooling, and readout:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Q-PRISM (Entangled PQC):} Full architecture with ring CNOT entanglement.
    \item \textbf{Separable Q-PRISM (No Entanglement):} Identical to (1) except that all CNOT gates are removed. Each qubit processes features independently.
    \item \textbf{Classical $\tanh$-MLP:} Replaces the PQC with a classical $\text{Linear}(13, 4) \to \text{SiLU} \to \text{Linear}(4, 8) \to \tanh$ head, producing identically bounded $(-1, 1)$ outputs with exactly 88 trainable parameters per head (matching the PQC parameter budget).
    \item \textbf{EGNN Param-Matched:} Standard classical EGNN with hidden dimension auto-searched to match $\sim 2{,}500$ parameters.
\end{enumerate}

