Table~\ref{tab:iso_ablation} summarizes the 20-seed comparison ($d_{\min} = 0.47$, $\alpha = 0.05$). Entanglement yields a detectable difference only on HOMO ($\Delta = 0.006$\,eV, $p = 0.002$, Cohen's $d = 0.85$). While $d = 0.85$ constitutes a statistically large effect by conventional thresholds, the absolute magnitude is practically insignificant relative to chemical accuracy: $0.006$\,eV represents a $2\%$ relative change, well within the $0.043$\,eV chemical accuracy margin. On LUMO ($p = 0.33$) and Gap ($p = 0.16$), entanglement provides no statistically significant gain. Removing entanglement entirely leaves the separable PQC substantially ahead of the classical $\tanh$-MLP: HOMO drops by $5.1\%$ ($p = 0.001$), LUMO drops by $12.2\%$ ($p < 10^{-7}$), and Gap drops by $13.0\%$ ($p < 10^{-8}$). These data indicate that single-qubit trigonometric rotation, rather than multi-qubit entanglement, generates the performance difference over standard MLPs.
We note that our multi-seed protocol confounds two sources of variance: data-split randomness and model initialization randomness. A full variance decomposition (e.g., via nested cross-validation with fixed splits) would isolate these contributions but is computationally prohibitive at the PQC simulation scale. As a qualitative bound, prior QM9 benchmarking studies~\cite{ramakrishnan2014quantum, Noe:2019yup} with fixed architectures and varying data splits report standard deviations of $\sim 0.01$--$0.02$\,eV on electronic targets at comparable training sizes, while our observed standard deviations across 20 seeds range from $0.016$--$0.047$\,eV (Table~\ref{tab:iso_ablation}). The modestly larger observed variance is consistent with the combined contribution of both sources, suggesting that data-split variance accounts for a substantial fraction but does not dominate. Crucially, all 20 paired differences between the key comparisons (e.g., Entangled vs.\ Separable on HOMO) are in the same direction, indicating that the observed effects are robust to both sources of variance.


\subsubsection{Functional Response Analysis: Capacity Starvation vs.\ Periodic Expressivity}

To visualize the mechanistic origin of this behavior, we extract edge-level response functions $f(\theta)$ from trained models by sweeping the 3-body bond angle $\theta \in [0^\circ, 180^\circ]$ at fixed interatomic distance ($d = 2.25$~\AA$^2$) and fixed node embeddings (training median).

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{figures/figure_angular_response.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Edge Functional Response vs.\ 3-Body Bond Angle} ($\theta \in [0^\circ, 180^\circ]$, fixed $d = 2.25$~\AA$^2$). Left: Entangled PQC outputs rich, multi-frequency trigonometric polynomials spanning $[-1, 1]$. Center: Separable PQC (no CNOTs) produces equally rich sinusoidal oscillations. Right: Classical $\tanh$-MLP outputs remain flat; at this parameter budget ($d_h = 4$), the network lacks the representational capacity to express oscillatory angular dependence.}
    \label{fig:angular_response}
\end{figure*}

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:angular_response}, the quantum circuits (both entangled and separable) produce smooth, large-amplitude sinusoidal oscillations across the angular domain.
Under the Fourier series framework of Schuld et al.~\cite{Schuld:2020enb} and Landman et al.~\cite{landman2023approximating}, each single-qubit rotation $R_Y(\theta) = \exp(-i \theta \sigma_y / 2)$ natively constructs Fourier basis elements $\cos(\theta)$ and $\sin(\theta)$ that compose into rich trigonometric polynomials across observable outputs.

The classical $\tanh$-MLP, by contrast, produces a flat response over the same angular range.
At this parameter budget ($d_h = 4$), the flat response reflects the hard representational limits of a 4-unit $\tanh$ network rather than NTK-regime spectral bias~\cite{rahaman2019spectral}, which describes learning dynamics in overparameterized networks.
A single $\sin$ neuron natively produces oscillations with one tunable frequency parameter; a $\tanh$ neuron is monotonic over most of its dynamic range, requiring multiple units to approximate each oscillation period.
With only 4 hidden units processing 13 mixed inputs, the classical $\tanh$-MLP suffers from \textit{expressive capacity starvation}: it cannot represent the oscillatory angular dependence that molecular electronic properties require.
While the spectral bias framework of Rahaman et al.~\cite{rahaman2019spectral} formally applies to overparameterized networks, the underlying principle---that periodic bases compactly represent oscillatory features that monotonic activations cannot efficiently express---extends to this low-parameter regime as an expressive capacity advantage.
This \textit{angular blindness} explains why standard classical baselines struggle on electronic orbital properties, which depend fundamentally on periodic angular overlap integrals.

When we examine PRISM (SIREN), the periodic sinusoidal activation $\sin(\omega_0 W x + b)$ directly supplies the compact oscillatory basis that the $\tanh$-MLP lacks~\cite{sitzmann2020siren}.
The classical SIREN head outputs the same large-amplitude, non-saturating oscillatory polynomials as the PQC, fully restoring angular sensitivity without requiring quantum circuits or statevector simulation.
This directly aligns with the \textbf{trainability-simulability dilemma} of Cerezo et al.~\cite{Cerezo:2023nqf}: because our 4-qubit PQC employs shallow depth and local observables to avoid barren plateaus, it resides in a classically simulable hypothesis class.
PRISM provides the explicit, practical classical architecture that captures this entire hypothesis class.
We emphasize that this alignment is \textit{motivational rather than a formal proof}: our experiments demonstrate that a classical SIREN network \textit{empirically captures} the PQC's learned representations at iso-parameter parity, but do not establish that the SIREN function class provably subsumes the PQC function class in the complexity-theoretic sense. The trainability-simulability dilemma provides theoretical motivation for expecting such classical simulability, but the gap between ``practically simulable'' and ``formally provably equivalent'' remains open.
One alternative explanation for PRISM's performance is gradient propagation: SIREN initialization~\cite{sitzmann2020siren} preserves variance across layers better than standard initialization for $\tanh$-MLPs. Our $2 \times 2$ factorial ablation (Section~\ref{sec:init_ablation}) directly isolates these factors: the periodic activation function provides the dominant improvement ($2\times$ larger effect than initialization), while initialization provides a secondary synergistic contribution. Additionally, Random Fourier Features (RFF) supply periodic bases yet fail to converge well (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), confirming that trainable periodic weights with learned frequency alignment, rather than arbitrary periodic bases or initialization dynamics alone, drive the improvement.


\subsubsection{Sample Complexity Scaling}

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_law}, Q-PRISM consistently exhibits superior generalization across all sample regimes on the HOMO-LUMO gap target, with the steepest improvement in the $N \in [100, 500]$ regime where classical models are most parameter-starved.

To test whether the trigonometric advantage is a low-data artifact, we evaluate all models across $N \in \{100, 5{,}000\}$ training molecules (3 seeds each) on HOMO with power-law fits $\text{MAE} \sim N^{-\alpha}$ (SM~J). Models with trigonometric expressivity (PQC and PRISM) maintain lower test MAE than the classical $\tanh$-MLP at \textit{every} sample size ($N = 100$: $0.441$ vs.\ $0.448$~eV; $N = 5{,}000$: $0.244$ vs.\ $0.258$~eV). Negligible generalization gaps ($< 0.01$~eV at $N \ge 500$) rule out the Caro et al.~\cite{Caro:2021mgf} ``unitary regularization'' hypothesis. Instead, the advantage manifests as an intercept shift in the log-log scaling plot ($\alpha_{\tanh} = 0.160$ vs.\ $\alpha_{\mathrm{PQC}} = 0.142$), consistent with a \textit{structural inductive bias} advantage fully accessible classically via PRISM.

