\subsection{Hardware and Circuit Analysis}

\paragraph{NISQ Hardware Noise and Theoretical Outlook.}
\label{sec:hardware_outlook}

\textit{All hardware noise analyses in this work are based on analytical noise models and calibrated simulator projections; no circuits were executed on physical quantum hardware. The projections presented below represent optimistic theoretical upper bounds based on published device specifications for architectures that do not yet exist in multi-qubit commercial hardware; actual performance may differ substantially due to crosstalk scaling, coherent control overheads, and fabrication variability.}
All PQC evaluations throughout the paper use PennyLane's \texttt{default.qubit} statevector simulator, corresponding to the infinite-shot (exact expectation value) limit. On physical hardware, finite shot budgets (Table~\ref{tab:resources}) would introduce measurement noise proportional to $1/\sqrt{N_{\mathrm{shots}}}$; the shot noise characterization in SM~E quantifies this impact and estimates the shot budget required for chemical-accuracy-preserving measurements.

We characterize Q-PRISM's projected deployment viability under per-gate depolarizing noise channels spanning $\lambda \in [10^{-5}, 5 \times 10^{-2}]$, covering next-generation superconducting processors (arm qubit, $\lambda \sim 10^{-5}$~\cite{Kline:2025ecg}) to current IBM transmon QPUs ($\lambda \sim 10^{-3}$~\cite{Kim:2023bwr}). Zero-Noise Extrapolation (ZNE) with noise scale factors $s \in [1.0, 3.0]$~\cite{Temme:2016vkz, Giurgica-Tiron:2020rcf} achieves $97.7\%$ error reduction at $\lambda = 0.005$ and $92.0\%$ at $\lambda = 0.010$. In the projected arm qubit regime ($\lambda \sim 10^{-5}$), raw unmitigated Q-PRISM expectations are projected to achieve sub-chemical-accuracy fidelity under idealized noise assumptions, potentially eliminating the need for error mitigation overhead. We emphasize that these projections represent \textit{optimistic theoretical upper bounds} based on published device specifications for architectures that do not yet exist in multi-qubit commercial hardware; actual performance may differ due to crosstalk scaling, coherent control overheads, and fabrication variability. Validation against a calibrated IBM Brisbane FakeBackend ($r = 0.87$, $p < 10^{-4}$) confirms structural agreement. Comprehensive noise characterization, ZNE analysis, parasitic $ZZ$ crosstalk impact, measurement readout noise ablations, and arm qubit crossing-point analysis are detailed in SM~E--F.

\paragraph{Circuit Depth and Qubit Scaling.}
\label{sec:circuit_scaling}

Evaluating Q-PRISM at variational depths $L \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16\}$ and qubit registers $n \in \{4, 6, 8\}$ (SM~G), deeper circuits yield substantial expressibility gains on the Gap target ($21.0\%$ MAE reduction from $L = 1$ to $L = 16$). Under projected arm qubit noise, performance is virtually indistinguishable from ideal simulation ($< 0.2\%$ degradation at $L = 16$); under transmon noise, the same depth degrades by $31.5\%$, exceeding the coherence window. Scaling to 8-qubit registers yields monotonic accuracy improvements ($6.6\%$ error reduction) while remaining within the arm qubit coherence budget ($\tau/T_2^E \approx 0.40\%$).

\paragraph{Continuous Symmetry Stress Testing.}
We verified exact $\mathrm{E}(3)$ equivariance via $1{,}000$ randomized rotations and translations: Q-PRISM achieves machine-precision invariance ($\sim 10^{-7}$--$10^{-8}$\,eV), comparable to classical EGNN, while a Non-Equivariant GNN fails catastrophically ($1.62$\,eV). Full results and visualizations are provided in SM~C.


\subsection{Additional Benchmarks: N-Body \& MD17}
\label{sec:additional_benchmarks}

We additionally validate Q-PRISM and PRISM on 3D charged N-body trajectory forecasting~\cite{satorras2022enequivariantgraphneural} and MD17 conformational energy prediction~\cite{chmielaMachineLearningAccurate2016} (SM~H--I). These benchmarks serve two important roles beyond QM9: (i) verifying that periodic activations do not introduce oscillatory artifacts on non-equilibrium geometries, and (ii) testing generalization to physical domains where the angular inductive bias may not confer an advantage.

On N-body dynamics ($5$ particles, Coulomb interaction), Q-PRISM maintains lower coordinate error than its parameter-matched Classical Twin ($0.6759$ vs.\ $0.6937$ MSE) while exhibiting no oscillatory artifacts from periodic activations. The smooth trajectory predictions confirm that the $\tanh$ output bounding and learned linear projections $\mathbf{W}_1$ successfully regularize the periodic basis in extrapolative regimes.

On MD17 (Ethanol and Aspirin), all models converge to similar energy MAEs ($\sim 3.3$\,kcal/mol for Ethanol), confirming that periodic activations produce smooth, physically reasonable potential energy surfaces on molecular dynamics conformations beyond the QM9 equilibrium regime. The absence of a periodic activation advantage on MD17 is consistent with the mechanistic interpretation: conformational energies are extensive properties governed by smooth, near-monotonic interatomic potentials, where the angular inductive bias of periodic activations provides no structural advantage. This target-specificity strengthens the claim that PRISM's improvement on QM9 electronic properties reflects genuine angular expressivity alignment rather than a generic capacity improvement.



