\paragraph{The Trainability-Simulability Dilemma.}
Table~\ref{tab:resources} summarizes the resource footprint and theoretical complexity across the baseline hierarchy.
Our empirical finding that PRISM (classical SIREN) matches and surpasses the 4-qubit PQC is grounded in recent quantum computational complexity theory.
Cerezo, Holmes et al.~\cite{Cerezo:2023nqf} proved that in variational quantum machine learning, the absence of barren plateaus is inextricably linked to classical simulability: any PQC that avoids barren plateaus via shallow circuit depth ($L \in \mathcal{O}(\log n)$) and local observables necessarily resides in a classically simulable hypothesis class.
In Q-PRISM, the edge PQC uses 4 qubits, $L = 2$ layers, and local single-qubit and nearest-neighbor correlator observables.
While this guarantees non-vanishing gradients and stable training, it simultaneously bounds the output to a low-dimensional truncated Fourier series~\cite{landman2023approximating, Schuld:2020enb}.
Conversely, attempting to escape classical simulability by scaling to deep, globally entangled circuits triggers exponential concentration of measure (Thanasilp et al.~\cite{Thanasilp:2022agd}), wherein quantum kernels concentrate to trivial constants and gradients vanish as $\mathcal{O}(2^{-n/2})$.
Thus, within the trainable regime for geometric graphs, quantum circuits provide no asymptotic expressivity separation over properly initialized classical periodic networks.

\paragraph{Macromolecular Scaling ($O(N)$).}
For molecules with $N > 50$ atoms, the fully-connected edge construction $O(N^2)$ becomes a bottleneck. Our implementation includes a $k$-nearest neighbor sparse graph builder that reduces edge complexity to $O(kN) = O(N)$ for constant $k$, enabling both Q-PRISM and PRISM to scale to macromolecular systems (proteins, polymers) without fundamentally altering the edge operator architecture.
For PRISM, each edge evaluation is a lightweight classical matrix multiplication and sine evaluation, executing in microseconds on modern GPUs without shot noise or quantum hardware latency.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

In this work, we investigated the mechanism of parameterized quantum circuits in $\mathrm{E}(3)$-equivariant graph neural networks for 3D molecular property prediction.
Through Q-PRISM, we initially observed a performance edge over standard classical baselines ($\tanh$-MLPs) on QM9 electronic properties (HOMO, LUMO, and Gap).
Systematic four-tier ablations across 20 random seeds showed that multi-qubit entanglement contributes a statistically detectable but practically inconsequential improvement. On HOMO, the effect size is statistically large (Cohen's $d = 0.85$, $\Delta = 0.006$\,eV, $p = 0.002$), yet the absolute magnitude falls well below the $0.043$\,eV chemical accuracy threshold. On LUMO and Gap, entanglement produces no significant change. The performance advantage traces directly to the trigonometric expressivity of single-qubit rotations.

By connecting this observation to the theory of periodic function approximation, we identified that standard classical MLPs with monotonic activations suffer from \textit{expressive capacity starvation} at low parameter budgets~\cite{rahaman2019spectral}: a $\tanh$ network with $d_h = 4$ hidden units cannot compactly represent the oscillatory angular dependence that molecular electronic properties require, whereas a single $\sin$ neuron natively produces oscillations.
Following an empirical dequantization approach inspired by Tang~\cite{Tang:2018lmw}, we resolved this classical limitation by introducing \textbf{PRISM}, the first equivariant message-passing graph neural network to incorporate periodic sinusoidal \textit{internal} activations with SIREN initialization~\cite{sitzmann2020siren}.
At exact iso-parameter matching ($2{,}527$ parameters), PRISM closely matches or surpasses the PQC across all electronic targets (HOMO: $0.269$ vs $0.303$\,eV; LUMO: $0.479$ vs $0.628$\,eV; Gap: $0.557$ vs $0.721$\,eV), while outperforming PaiNN on 4 out of 6 QM9 properties at iso-parameter budgets.
A $2 \times 2$ factorial ablation across 10 random seeds (Section~\ref{sec:init_ablation}) decomposes the mechanism: the periodic $\sin$ activation is the primary contributor (main effect: $-0.16$ to $-0.18$\,eV across LUMO and Gap, Cohen's $d = 4.9$--$9.4$), with SIREN initialization providing a secondary synergistic effect ($-0.07$ to $-0.09$\,eV). Neither factor alone matches the full PRISM architecture, confirming that the combination constitutes a genuinely novel architectural contribution rather than an artifact of either component.
Crucially, the periodic advantage does not diminish at larger parameter budgets: scaling from $d_h = 4$ ($2{,}527$ params) to $d_h = 64$ ($29{,}599$ params) increases PRISM's advantage over the Classical Twin from $+25\%$ to $+49\%$ on LUMO (Section~\ref{sec:param_scaling}), demonstrating that periodic activations provide a structural inductive bias that cannot be compensated by increasing monotonic network capacity.
Fourier spectral analysis confirms that PQC and PRISM edge outputs share similar frequency structure (cosine similarity $= 0.90$, $100\%$ top-5 frequency overlap; Section~\ref{sec:spectral_comparison}), supporting the empirical dequantization interpretation.
Full statistical multiplicity correction via Benjamini-Hochberg False Discovery Rate ($m = 48$ hypotheses) confirms that periodic activations provide a statistically significant advantage over standard $\tanh$ ($q < 0.02$).

These findings align with the trainability-simulability dilemma~\cite{Cerezo:2023nqf} and exponential concentration bounds~\cite{Thanasilp:2022agd}. Specifically, our empirical dequantization result is established for 4-qubit parameterized circuits with $\le$4 variational layers, ring CNOT entanglement topology, and local single-qubit and nearest-neighbor correlator observables. For such shallow PQCs, apparent performance advantages over classical baselines reflect periodic inductive biases that are readily reproducible on classical hardware. Full-scale evaluation on the complete QM9 dataset ($N = 110{,}000$) demonstrates that the periodic activation advantage persists---narrowing from $\sim 30\%$ at $N = 1{,}000$ to $\sim 10$--$11\%$ at $N = 110{,}000$ but never closing---confirming a structural inductive bias rather than a low-data artifact.
We acknowledge that no serious claim for quantum computational advantage exists for 4-qubit, low-depth, low-entanglement circuits; our contribution is therefore not the disproof of an active quantum advantage claim, but rather (i) the constructive identification of the \textit{mechanism} (trigonometric expressivity) underlying PQC performance on molecular tasks, and (ii) the introduction of PRISM as a practical, parameter-efficient classical architecture that brings periodic sinusoidal internal activations into equivariant message-passing GNNs for the first time. Whether quantum circuits with deeper registers ($L > 4$), higher qubit counts ($n \ge 16$), non-local observables, or more expressive entanglement topologies can break this classical parity remains an open theoretical and experimental question.
In doing so, this work provides (i) a reusable benchmarking methodology for testing putative QML advantages via inductive-bias-matched classical surrogates, and (ii) PRISM, a parameter-efficient classical architecture for molecular geometric deep learning.

\paragraph{Limitations and Future Work.}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Parameter-constrained evaluation regime:} Our primary evaluation benchmarks models at $N = 1{,}000$ molecules with $\sim 2{,}500$ parameters. Full-scale evaluation extends to $N = 110{,}000$ (Table~\ref{tab:full_scale}), confirming a persistent $10$--$11\%$ periodic activation advantage. Parameter scaling experiments (Table~\ref{tab:param_scaling}) demonstrate that the periodic advantage persists---and in fact grows---when the hidden dimension is increased from $d_h = 4$ to $d_h = 64$ ($29{,}599$ parameters). Nevertheless, our largest models remain below the capacity of state-of-the-art architectures (e.g., PaiNN at $100$k+ parameters, MACE at $1$M+ parameters), and evaluation on datasets beyond QM9 (e.g., OC20, ANI-1x) remains future work.
    \item \textbf{3-body angular pooling (M5):} To maintain linear $O(E)$ complexity, our architecture averages angles over third atoms: $\bar{\theta}_{ij}$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:angle}). While computationally efficient, this averaging discards individual triplet geometry, unlike DimeNet++~\cite{klicperaFastUncertaintyAwareDirectional2020} or GemNet~\cite{klicperaGemNetUniversalDirectional2021}. Future work may explore per-triplet periodic projections.
    \item \textbf{Scalar-only geometric representation:} Both Q-PRISM and PRISM project 3D directional geometry into scalar invariants (interatomic distances $d_{ij}$ and averaged bond angles $\bar{\theta}_{ij}$), discarding per-edge directional vector information. As a consequence, the models cannot distinguish chiral enantiomers or capture higher-order multipolar interactions that require explicit vector or tensor equivariant channels~\cite{schuttEquivariantMessagePassing2021, batatiaMACEHigherOrder2022}. Extending PRISM to coupled scalar-vector message passing (analogous to PaiNN) or higher-order tensor equivariant architectures (analogous to MACE) is a natural future direction.
    \item \textbf{PRISM-PaiNN hybrid architecture:} The complementary performance profiles of PRISM (superior on HOMO, Dipole, Polarizability) and PaiNN (superior on LUMO, Gap) suggest a natural architectural synthesis: replacing SiLU activations in PaiNN's scalar interaction branch with SIREN periodic activations while preserving vector equivariant update channels. Since the periodic activation acts only on the invariant scalar pathway, this requires no modification to PaiNN's equivariance guarantees. A practical consideration is gradient stability: the Lipschitz constant of $\sin(\omega_0 x)$ scales as $\omega_0$, so the scalar-to-vector gating mechanism must accommodate amplified gradients. We leave experimental validation of this hybrid to future work.
    \item \textbf{Hardware execution \& topology constraints (m1, m2):} All quantum circuits were simulated via PennyLane statevector backends; no physical 4-qubit fluxonium ring processor currently exists in commercial hardware. Scaling beyond 4 qubits on planar superconducting lattices (such as IBM's heavy-hex) also introduces SWAP routing overhead, increasing effective circuit depth by roughly $1.5$--$2.0\times$.
    \item \textbf{NISQ execution overhead \& hybrid training (m3):} Sequential QPU edge evaluations incur measurement shot noise and latency ($3$--$12$\,s per molecule). Near-term deployment could benefit from hybrid training: pre-training the equivariant backbone classically with PRISM, followed by quantum fine-tuning on targeted subsets to minimize QPU execution time.
    \item \textbf{Noiseless training assumption (m4):} Hardware noise was characterized post-hoc via ZNE and analytical noise projections. Noise-aware training~\cite{cincio2021machine, Sharma:2020qjq} incorporating device-calibrated density matrices via PennyLane's \texttt{default.mixed} device represents a natural next step for experimental deployment.
    \item \textbf{Shallow PQC scope:} Our empirical dequantization result is established for shallow, low-qubit PQCs (4 qubits, $L \le 4$, ring CNOT topology) with local Pauli observables. We do not provide a formal proof that the SIREN function class subsumes the PQC function class in the complexity-theoretic sense; our evidence is empirical (iso-parameter performance matching and spectral similarity). Whether the result extends to deeper circuits ($L > 4$) with non-local observables, higher qubit counts ($n \ge 16$), or more expressive entanglement topologies (e.g., all-to-all, heavy-hex native) remains an open question. Such circuits may access function classes beyond the reach of classical periodic models, though the trainability-simulability dilemma~\cite{Cerezo:2023nqf} suggests that trainable circuits will remain classically simulable.
\end{enumerate}

